Q: How to handle duplicate IDs for inline SVGs? A: <p><strong>No need for JS or PHP</strong> - you can use <code>mask-image</code> in your global CSS</p>
<p>1. Use a regular fill color in the SVG.<br />
2. Add a class to its wrapper.<br />
3. Apply the gradient using <code>::after</code> and <code>mask-image</code> in your site’s global CSS.</p>
<p>Here's an example for social media icons that I used multiple times in my page:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.sara-icon {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-flex;
  width: 90px;
  height: 90px;
}

.sara-icon &gt; svg {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}

.sara-instagram-gradient::after,
.sara-facebook-gradient::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  -webkit-mask-position: center;
  -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
  -webkit-mask-size: 100% 100%;
  mask-position: center;
  mask-repeat: no-repeat;
  mask-size: 100% 100%;
}

.sara-instagram-gradient::after {
  background:
    radial-gradient(ellipse 65.062% 65.062% at 57.674% 85.18%, transparent 64%, #8c3aaa 100%),
    radial-gradient(ellipse 82.552% 82.552% at 27.579% 82.673%, #fa8f21 9%, #d82d7e 78%);
  -webkit-mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml;base64,");
  mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml;base64,");
}

.sara-facebook-gradient::after {
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #01b0ff 0%, #0171ff 100%);
  -webkit-mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml;base64,");
  mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml;base64,");
}

